WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.435
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.410
MARC PAWLIGER: So how did
we get to where we are?

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:10.705
So Google's big.

00:00:10.705 --> 00:00:14.280
It's not really a surprise, but
we have hundreds of buildings.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.670
We have tens of
millions of square feet

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:19.830
on all of our offices,
and all of our labs.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.120
And we have difficulty
managing that.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.880
So there's existing
tools and platforms.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.620
Have difficulty scaling.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:31.090
Data standardization in
practice is difficult.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:32.750
So what we've done
is we've built

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:35.600
tools that allow easy, fast,
and accurate commissioning

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:36.500
at scale.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.300
Because when you think about
it, building management today

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:42.710
is really fine, right?

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.510
And the answer is,
well, not really.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.240
So we built this
platform in order

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.550
to manage our buildings better.

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.850
And fundamentally, we want
to run Google on Google.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:56.120
So we want to be a customer of
ourselves so that we can show,

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.760
this is how we manage our
data and our buildings.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:01.370
So we want to be able
to access our data,

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:02.900
we want to be able
to analyze it,

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:04.562
and we want to be
able to act on it.

00:01:04.562 --> 00:01:06.770
So today, we're going to
talk about the platform, how

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.893
we built it, some of the
data science behind it,

00:01:08.893 --> 00:01:11.060
and then we're going to
show you a little quick demo

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.590
of the kind of applications
that you can build on it.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:16.970
So today, when you're thinking
about building management,

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.070
there's really three personas.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:20.720
The first one is,
if you're either

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.210
the landlord or the sole tenant.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.010
So you're caring
about things at scale.

00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:27.080
You may not care about
an individual building,

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.460
but you care about how, for
example, the energy spend

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.350
and the space utilization
happens at scale,

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:36.860
and why this building is working
better than that building.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.265
You may also be the
facilities ops manager.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:40.640
That's a little
bit more focused.

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.280
You care about a
particular building,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.480
or a particular campus.

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.150
And you want to know how you
can actually operate it better.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.680
You might be dealing
with trouble tickets.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.222
You might be doing truck rolls
in order to do the maintenance

00:01:51.222 --> 00:01:52.700
and so forth.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.530
And then there's the occupant.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:58.370
So all of you here today
probably work in an office.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.370
And you care about having
the building be comfortable.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:03.890
You care about the
facilities being

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:07.560
able to let you get your work
done efficiently, effectively,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:10.500
and comfortably.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.340
So what's the opportunity here?

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.442
Well, so first is energy.

00:02:14.442 --> 00:02:15.900
That's the first
one that, any time

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.567
someone's talking
about smart buildings,

00:02:17.567 --> 00:02:19.465
they really hone in on.

00:02:19.465 --> 00:02:21.090
You want to be able
to use less energy,

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:23.490
and you want to
spend less on it.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.440
And I don't think
we really need to go

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.380
into too much detail about
the motivation behind there.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.460
It's both on cost.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.530
It's on efficiency.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.150
It's being more green.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:35.700
If you're on the
operational side,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:38.250
I don't think it's uncommon
to say that people really

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.362
get alarm fatigue.

00:02:40.362 --> 00:02:42.570
If you have anything to do
with operating a building,

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.695
you know that there are
literally tens of thousands

00:02:44.695 --> 00:02:46.950
of alerts that come in,
either weekly or monthly,

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:48.390
that are just routinely ignored.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.330
You don't have the time to
be able to dig into the ones

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.480
that you really have to
take care of in order

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.360
to address issues that you
have either with occupants,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.360
or with your facility overall.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.730
And they hide efficiencies
that you might otherwise

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:07.430
be able to realize if you
were able to dig deeper.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:09.900
And then, on the
productivity side,

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.430
answering hot and
cold tickets, that

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.143
means people are
uncomfortable enough not

00:03:13.143 --> 00:03:14.560
to be able to get
their work done,

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.935
and they're telling
you about it,

00:03:15.935 --> 00:03:18.090
if you're managing the facility.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:21.540
And moreover, Google
has tons of buildings.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.560
Some of them are
very complex layouts.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:24.810
People have a lot of meetings.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.900
And we have found that people
actually spend-- and this

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.870
is a measured amount--
millions of hours searching

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.625
on the floor for the meeting
room that they need to get to.

00:03:33.625 --> 00:03:35.250
Now, there may be
the issue that you're

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:36.370
having too many meetings.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:37.578
That's a separate discussion.

00:03:37.578 --> 00:03:38.970
But fundamentally,
you need to be

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.527
able to get people to where
they need to go faster.

00:03:42.527 --> 00:03:44.610
And then there's something
that is very important.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.537
I'm not going to be able to go
into too much about it today,

00:03:47.537 --> 00:03:48.495
but it's cybersecurity.

00:03:48.495 --> 00:03:51.240
You want to make sure that
the devices that you have--

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.340
and it's a growing set of
them-- in your building,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:57.390
whether that's HVAC,
so climate control,

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:59.790
whether it's occupancy
sensors, whether it's

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.550
different kinds of alarms,
water, life safety, that

00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:04.380
kind of thing, all
of them are safe,

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.270
and they're communicating
in such a way

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:09.300
that they're only communicating
the way that they should,

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.520
and that somebody
isn't going to be

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.727
able to compromise your network
through your air conditioner.

00:04:15.727 --> 00:04:17.310
So there's lots of
stories that you've

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:20.550
heard about that
in the news, and we

00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:23.220
don't want the systems that
we're building over here

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.100
to be one of those vectors.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.770
So our platform is
really to the rescue.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.320
And the nut of it
really is, we want

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:36.960
to have the ability to stop
having people be your sensors.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.400
You want to be
able to use sensors

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.983
that you have in the
building, and you

00:04:39.983 --> 00:04:42.360
want to gain the
intelligence from that.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.980
So on the energy
efficiency side,

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.650
you want to be able to gather
and aggregate all your data.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.100
You want to be able to use
machine learning, ML, to detect

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:53.520
anomalous behavior so
that you can address it.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.470
On the efficiency
side, you want to be

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.990
able to filter all of those
tens of thousands of alarms

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.200
that you unfortunately
aren't unable to get to,

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.520
to get to the small
number of them

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.270
that you actually want
to dispatch a tech

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.110
or dispatch a truck to
actually take care of.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.035
And when you have
people who go on-site

00:05:09.035 --> 00:05:10.410
to address these
issues, you want

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.535
them to be better informed.

00:05:11.535 --> 00:05:13.523
You want them to know
where their devices are,

00:05:13.523 --> 00:05:14.940
what's actually
going on, and what

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:18.520
could be the root cause of
causing them to malfunction.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:21.240
On the occupant
side, you want to be

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.030
able to not wait for the
trouble ticket to open up.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.890
You want to be able to
detect that something

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:33.180
is sufficiently out of band to
be able to proactively address

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:33.720
it.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.850
And you want to be able
to definitively get people

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:37.350
to where they want to go.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.040
So they shouldn't be lost.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.185
They shouldn't waste
time where you're

00:05:41.185 --> 00:05:42.810
waiting for that last
person to show up

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.145
for your discussion
or your meeting.

00:05:45.145 --> 00:05:46.520
And then on the
security side, we

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.600
want to be continually
monitoring these devices

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.750
to make sure that they're
only communicating in a way,

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.760
and only communicating
to the devices

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.300
that they are supposed to be.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:57.960
And like I said, we can talk
more about that afterwards

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.910
if you're interested in
that particular aspect

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:02.160
of the presentation.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:06.210
So today's building management
systems are really siloed.

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:07.740
They've been around
for a long time.

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:09.407
But fundamentally,
you're going to have,

00:06:09.407 --> 00:06:11.520
for each one of the
different silos--

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.352
whether it's AV, or energy,
or lighting, or security,

00:06:14.352 --> 00:06:15.810
they're going to
have their own UI.

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:17.970
They're going to have their own
stack, their own storage, which

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:18.900
may be on prem.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:21.450
It may be in a
third-party cloud.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:25.147
It may be through an API
to access the devices.

00:06:25.147 --> 00:06:27.480
They're going to have their
own networking requirements.

00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:29.760
And really, they're separate.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.710
They also tend to
be very rule-based.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.060
They don't tend to be learning.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:36.210
So it makes it difficult, if
you get your rules set up just

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.850
so for one building, to be
able to take them and put them

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.710
into another building.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:42.360
It's basically a lot
of walled gardens,

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:44.580
and that's problematic.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.610
And today, it's also
sometimes difficult

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:50.040
to be able to take all
of these separate silos

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.112
and combine them into a more
cohesive orthogonal platform

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:54.570
where you're able
to access things,

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:57.780
and apply the same kind of
intelligence to one system

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.770
as to another.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:01.940
So what's our approach?

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:03.980
This is the platform
that we want to build.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:06.290
It really orchestrates
all of this together.

00:07:06.290 --> 00:07:08.210
And it starts with
the fact that we

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.367
want to be able to take
any kind of device--

00:07:10.367 --> 00:07:12.200
like I said, whether
it's a climate control,

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.320
whether it's a sensor,
whether it's security--

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.830
and think about it
in an orthogonal way.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:20.480
So we want to be
able to have an API

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.880
that your mental model of
how you think about all these

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:24.150
is the same.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:28.550
So at the bottom, you have
your devices, and your network,

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:29.900
and your connectivity.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:32.962
So fundamentally, that's not
what we're going to go into.

00:07:32.962 --> 00:07:35.420
We assume that you're going to
have some kind of a gateway.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:37.520
You're going to have some
kind of IP connectivity

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:39.230
so that you can talk
to these devices

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:40.550
and communicate with them.

00:07:40.550 --> 00:07:43.970
And they're going to use any
kind of industry standards--

00:07:43.970 --> 00:07:45.590
Modbus, BACnet, et cetera--

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:46.670
to communicate with you.

00:07:46.670 --> 00:07:48.990
And we're assuming that you
have all of that set up.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:53.225
That's kind of a standard
understood way to do things.

00:07:53.225 --> 00:07:55.100
But then on top of it,
what we're going to do

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:58.010
is we're going to take the data
from each one of the devices

00:07:58.010 --> 00:07:59.420
and put it in a data lake.

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:01.310
Now, when I say data lake to
you, that means a lot of things

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:02.420
to different people.

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:05.530
But fundamentally, to us,
it's a time series database

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:09.650
stamped for each one of the
devices, and done in such a way

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:13.130
that we understand the ontology,
the relationship of the devices

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:14.990
to each other, have
generalized them

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:17.540
to different classes
of devices, and we're

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:20.360
able to access them
in a structured way.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.970
Sometimes, a data lake can be
thought of as just a collection

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:23.470
of data.

00:08:23.470 --> 00:08:26.520
But here, we're putting
order on top of it.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:29.060
And we do that using a
building ontology and metadata

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:30.410
about each one of the devices.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:34.063
And Charbel will go into much
more detail about that later.

00:08:34.063 --> 00:08:35.480
And another thing
that we do which

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:37.772
is, I think, a little bit
different than other systems,

00:08:37.772 --> 00:08:40.520
is we have spatial understanding
of where these devices are,

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:42.395
where they are in
relationship to each other.

00:08:42.395 --> 00:08:44.900
Floor maps for the
building so that not only

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:46.400
do we know what
the device is, what

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:49.010
it's connected to, its
relationship to other devices,

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:51.178
but where it is as well.

00:08:51.178 --> 00:08:52.970
And of course, none of
this would be useful

00:08:52.970 --> 00:08:56.330
unless you had APIs in
order to access the data.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:08:58.940
And we do so using the
same kind of mental model

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:03.960
that you do for all of the other
Google Cloud Platform stacks

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.833
and technologies that we have.

00:09:06.833 --> 00:09:09.000
This is something that we
did because it makes sense

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.610
to do it in a standardized way
so that other developers who

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.158
are developing
apps on top of this

00:09:13.158 --> 00:09:14.700
are able to access
it in the same way

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:18.310
that they do any of the other
cloud platform technologies.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.400
And altogether, this
is what we're calling

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:22.950
the digital building Platform.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:29.440
So taken altogether, that's the
special black box, if you will,

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.290
that takes in the
data on one side

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.430
and provides it to
applications on the top.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:37.560
So this is where we've worked
with some third parties

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:41.670
to take their existing systems
that do things like alarming,

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:45.600
and analysis, and so forth,
and they use our platform

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:46.980
as the source of their data.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:48.960
Or we also have some
groups inside Google

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.390
that have built
first-party applications

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:56.697
to do things like anomaly
detection and alarming as well.

00:09:56.697 --> 00:09:58.030
So how do we onboard a building?

00:09:58.030 --> 00:09:59.800
Now, what does onboarding mean?

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:04.090
So onboarding-- for anyone who's
set up a building automation

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:05.770
or a building
management system--

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:08.950
is taking your devices and
being able to talk to them,

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.860
and getting them connected
into whatever kind

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:16.090
of management software or
platform that you have.

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.160
And it usually is a
very painful process.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:20.660
Sometimes it can day take days.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.390
Sometimes it can take weeks.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:26.195
Well, with the platform that
we built, we started there.

00:10:26.195 --> 00:10:28.570
And it actually did take us
on the order of days or weeks

00:10:28.570 --> 00:10:29.330
to onboard a building.

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:30.760
But we actually got
it down to the point

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:32.385
where we can do it
in a matter of hours

00:10:32.385 --> 00:10:34.390
for most of the buildings
in Google, which is

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:36.580
a pretty big impressive thing.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:38.200
So we start where
you either have

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:40.450
greenfield, which
are new devices,

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:42.760
or brownfield, which
are existing devices.

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:44.860
And those devices
might be individual.

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.710
So they might be something
like an air handler.

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:51.130
They might be something that
you think of in aggregate

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:54.117
together where you have
different component devices--

00:10:54.117 --> 00:10:56.200
like a VAV, or something
like that-- inside of it,

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.410
and you think of them as one.

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:02.950
And then we create an idealized
representation of them.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:06.280
So you have one where you think
of each one of the classes

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:08.980
of devices in a way
that it encompasses

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.080
all of the special
fields and data

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:13.330
points that each one of
the manufacturers' devices

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:14.242
might have in it.

00:11:14.242 --> 00:11:15.700
But we do it in
such a way that you

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:17.680
can address them orthogonally.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:19.660
So you don't have to
worry about what brand

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:22.543
this is, what type
this is, and so forth.

00:11:22.543 --> 00:11:25.210
And so if you've ever dealt with
these devices, each one of them

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:26.300
has data points.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:28.630
That's a piece of data and
a tag that basically says,

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:30.172
what is this, what
does it represent,

00:11:30.172 --> 00:11:31.930
what units are it
in, and so forth.

00:11:31.930 --> 00:11:33.670
And in the idealized
representation,

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:34.840
we have the same thing.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:37.010
And we do this in
such a way, again,

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:39.550
to make it so that you can
treat all of the devices

00:11:39.550 --> 00:11:41.950
in a particular class the same.

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:44.500
And then we need to figure
out, for each one of these data

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:48.460
points, what semantic tag, what
tells you what this data is

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:52.330
and what it represents, what
unit it has, and so forth.

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:53.980
So for example,
this might be a tag

00:11:53.980 --> 00:11:56.710
that you have in
the actual device.

00:11:56.710 --> 00:11:58.480
And here are the
different semantic tags

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:00.430
that we might
break it down into,

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:03.970
because we can do clustering
and matching to basically say,

00:12:03.970 --> 00:12:07.000
we recognize this kind of
device in this set of points

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:10.150
that it has, and this is how
we map it to a set of tags.

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:12.578
And then, basically,
we have a mechanism

00:12:12.578 --> 00:12:14.620
that will go through and
do that matching for all

00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:17.980
of the other points
and tags in the system.

00:12:17.980 --> 00:12:20.770
And that way, it saves
a lot of the manual work

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:23.500
that you used to have
to do before to do it

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:26.177
in a more automatic way.

00:12:26.177 --> 00:12:27.760
And then, of course,
you have the APIs

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:30.730
to access it on the top,
and then the applications.

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:32.655
So in whole, this
is basically what

00:12:32.655 --> 00:12:34.030
the stack looks
like after you've

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:37.720
gotten something onboarded
and into the building system.

00:12:40.300 --> 00:12:42.480
So what do we do with all
this once you're done?

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:44.813
It's all great to have the
data, but unless you actually

00:12:44.813 --> 00:12:47.350
make use of it, it's
not that interesting.

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:51.600
So just as an example,
particular problem

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:55.740
that you might face is an
outer set point temperature.

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:57.840
So if you look at
the box in yellow,

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:00.810
that's what the data looks like
raw coming from the device.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:03.750
And it says that
this is a sensor that

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:07.270
should be in the range from 62
Fahrenheit to 72 Fahrenheit.

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:11.310
It's currently 81 degrees, so
obviously, that's problematic.

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:13.380
This has landed
in the data lake.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:16.610
The sensor data captured the
data, and it put it in there.

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.423
It tagged it with a time stamp.

00:13:19.423 --> 00:13:21.090
None of this is really
unusual if you've

00:13:21.090 --> 00:13:24.370
dealt with any typical building
automation system before.

00:13:24.370 --> 00:13:27.000
But we also have the ontology
and the mapping which tells you

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:33.030
where the device is, what
type it is, what kind of class

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:38.250
it relates to, and allows us to
get more contextual information

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:41.610
about this particular error,
such that when we then dispatch

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:43.710
someone to take
care of it, they're

00:13:43.710 --> 00:13:45.840
able to have more
of this context,

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.300
and hopefully are able to
address the problem faster

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:49.780
than they otherwise would.

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:51.717
So the whole is greater
the sum of the parts.

00:13:51.717 --> 00:13:53.550
And you can use all of
these kind of sensors

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:56.143
together to tell
an aggregate story.

00:13:56.143 --> 00:13:57.810
Now I'm going to
switch over to Charbel,

00:13:57.810 --> 00:13:59.810
who's going to tell you
some of the data science

00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:02.065
behind all of this platform.

00:14:02.065 --> 00:14:02.940
CHARBEL KAED: Thanks.

00:14:10.230 --> 00:14:13.940
So I want to talk about, how
do we make sense of this data?

00:14:13.940 --> 00:14:15.530
As Marc mentioned
before, we have

00:14:15.530 --> 00:14:20.810
all of these silos that
are really in verticals

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:22.520
where the data is trapped.

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:25.730
So if you take a step back, we
have really different devices

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:29.600
and systems that are
provided by various vendors.

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:33.200
And they can vary by geographic
region, or by country,

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:37.500
because no single vendor
can cover the whole planet.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:40.110
And as we mentioned, the
data is trapped in silos.

00:14:40.110 --> 00:14:42.570
We saw we have the lighting,
we have the cooling,

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:44.060
the badge access reader.

00:14:44.060 --> 00:14:47.840
All of these data are
really in vertical.

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:50.090
And of course, when you're
talking about the building,

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:52.610
it's a whole
ecosystem, so we need

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:55.600
to do data correlation
for better management.

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:58.070
If we know that the
space is not occupied,

00:14:58.070 --> 00:14:59.660
we don't have to clean it.

00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:01.610
Or maybe we don't
have to cool it.

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:03.810
Or maybe we don't
have to light it.

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:07.130
So all of these
simple information

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:12.890
can give us a lot of insights
to better managed space.

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:16.730
And of course, we want to
be able to correlate data

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:19.490
so we can serve it for
these three personas,

00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:22.910
whether you are a landlord,
an occupant, or a facility

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:24.350
manager.

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:29.940
So one of the ways of doing
that is by relying on ontology.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:32.390
So before I go through
it, by show of hands,

00:15:32.390 --> 00:15:36.160
who heard about
this word before?

00:15:36.160 --> 00:15:36.660
OK.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.810
Cool.

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:41.510
So an ontology is
a common vocabulary

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:44.120
to refer to the
concepts in a building.

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:47.360
But we can also use it as a
way to express the relationship

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:49.790
between these concepts.

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:51.590
Don't frown upon
this definition.

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:53.165
I will go through more details.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:15:58.240
So this is something
we didn't invent.

00:15:58.240 --> 00:16:00.430
It started with the
Greek philosophers

00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:02.780
when they started
to map the world.

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:05.780
How a mammal can
relate to a human.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:10.513
How a living being is related
to the planet, and all of that.

00:16:10.513 --> 00:16:11.930
And of course,
computer scientists

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:15.030
like to reuse things
from the past.

00:16:15.030 --> 00:16:18.140
So computer scientists
reuse ontology

00:16:18.140 --> 00:16:20.790
as a way to represent
information.

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:24.180
It can be also in a building.

00:16:24.180 --> 00:16:26.570
So in a simplified
definition, an ontology

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.600
is just a way to
represent domain concepts

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:32.370
and the relationship
between these concepts.

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:34.940
They are usually
represented in triplets,

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:37.830
and we distinguish between
classes and instances.

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:39.920
So you can see we have a
city and a country, which

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:43.610
are classes, connected by
its "located in," which

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:44.870
is a relationship.

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:46.820
And then we have the
instances, like Paris

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:49.640
is located in France.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:51.800
What is nice about
ontologies is we don't need

00:16:51.800 --> 00:16:54.890
to invent how do
we represent them,

00:16:54.890 --> 00:16:57.350
because the World Wide
Web Consortium already

00:16:57.350 --> 00:16:59.510
proposed standardization--

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:02.300
like RDF, OWL, and JSON-LD--

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:06.510
to represent and express
these kind of relationships.

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:09.089
So let's take an
example in a building.

00:17:09.089 --> 00:17:11.359
We have a location,
which is a concept.

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:14.510
We can have subcategories, like
a building floor and a meeting

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.180
rooms that are also
connected together.

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:20.210
And we can also instantiate
them with a room, a floor,

00:17:20.210 --> 00:17:22.020
and a specific building.

00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:25.190
So this can be seen
as one of the silos.

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:28.580
And we also have another
silo, which is the devices.

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.210
We have a sensor
that is measuring

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:33.860
a quantity, a temperature
sensor measuring a temperature,

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:35.840
and then we have the instances.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.240
So as you can see,
thanks to the ontology,

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.000
we can link these
two silos together

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:43.865
by adding relationship and
connecting them together.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:50.280
So there's a lot of
ontologies out there.

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:52.950
We didn't want to reinvent
the wheel at Google,

00:17:52.950 --> 00:17:56.060
so we looked at
what is out there.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:57.980
I'm going to focus
on the following

00:17:57.980 --> 00:18:00.210
just on three of them.

00:18:00.210 --> 00:18:02.660
And as you can see,
with the cloud tags,

00:18:02.660 --> 00:18:08.180
there's really a lot, like
SAREF, and KNX, M2M ontology.

00:18:08.180 --> 00:18:11.000
I'm going to focus
only on three of them.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:15.170
So the Haystack Vocabulary
started in 2014,

00:18:15.170 --> 00:18:19.430
and it was one of the first
efforts that said, OK, I'm

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:22.670
going to start proposing a
vocabulary for my building

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:23.870
domain.

00:18:23.870 --> 00:18:27.290
And this is where we started
to see these tags emerging.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:32.000
So it's a tag-based system
similar to the Twitter hashtag

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:35.870
where, for a certain point,
like a temperature sensor 1,

00:18:35.870 --> 00:18:39.747
you can assign one or
many tags together.

00:18:39.747 --> 00:18:40.830
So it's a tag-based model.

00:18:40.830 --> 00:18:42.360
It's not an ontology.

00:18:42.360 --> 00:18:46.880
And back in 2014, they didn't
rely on W3C consortium,

00:18:46.880 --> 00:18:51.630
but they had their
own Zinc standard.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:56.870
Another interesting progress
was provided by Siemens

00:18:56.870 --> 00:19:00.530
in 2015, which points
out in the research paper

00:19:00.530 --> 00:19:02.790
the limitation of Haystack.

00:19:02.790 --> 00:19:04.040
The lack of expressivity.

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:06.140
The ambiguity in assigning tags.

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:09.110
You can imagine that you cannot
express relationship with just

00:19:09.110 --> 00:19:10.070
tags.

00:19:10.070 --> 00:19:13.287
It becomes really
very hard to manage.

00:19:13.287 --> 00:19:14.870
And of course, you
need an abstraction

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:16.540
when you do queries.

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.855
The tags are a very flat system.

00:19:19.855 --> 00:19:21.230
When you are trying
to query, you

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:25.200
might want to query by
location, or by an upper class.

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:28.490
So you lose that with the
tags because they are flat.

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:32.060
So Siemens proposed the
Haystack Tagging Ontology,

00:19:32.060 --> 00:19:34.520
which proposes to mix
between the Haystack

00:19:34.520 --> 00:19:36.740
tags, the semantic
sensor network

00:19:36.740 --> 00:19:43.100
ontology, and the building
models like BIM and IFC.

00:19:43.100 --> 00:19:45.020
So basically, what
Siemens tried to do

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:51.265
is to take these flat tags
and represent it into triples.

00:19:51.265 --> 00:19:54.650
But as you can see,
it's not really

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:57.350
a lot of explicit
relationship just

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:00.080
to add has tag in the triplets.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:02.540
We still lack of expressivity.

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:06.025
And this is where Bricks
come into the picture.

00:20:06.025 --> 00:20:07.400
So it's the Brick
schema proposed

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:13.340
by IBM and several
academic universities which

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:17.450
took into consideration
Haystack, Haystack Tagging

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:19.830
Ontology, and
SAREF, and proposed

00:20:19.830 --> 00:20:25.836
a model that is, I would say,
richer than just a simple tag

00:20:25.836 --> 00:20:27.470
model.

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:29.690
And it's also relied on
a pragmatic approach.

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:32.000
They took around
six buildings that

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.450
are scattered across
the globe, that they

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:36.650
support with different vendors.

00:20:36.650 --> 00:20:39.140
And they try to refine,
what are the concept

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:43.150
or the common concepts of these
ontologies and their proposed

00:20:43.150 --> 00:20:45.230
Brick.

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:50.060
What is nice about Brick is
that it's not just out there.

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:56.570
It is part now of
the [INAUDIBLE] body

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:59.760
which is trying to push
Brick to become a standard.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:03.200
So this is really
an interesting thing

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:05.210
you are into buildings
domain, because you

00:21:05.210 --> 00:21:10.400
can go and influence this
vocabulary and this ontology.

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:13.640
And of course, Brick
schema guided our choice

00:21:13.640 --> 00:21:15.950
for our digital
building platform

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.830
because of its
richness, and that it

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:24.300
builds on prior work
related to the ontology.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:28.130
So now that I talked about how
do we represent information,

00:21:28.130 --> 00:21:34.310
I would like to talk
about what are the Bricks

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:38.670
that we reused from GCP
to build this platform.

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:42.560
So we have a cloud IoT
to connect the devices

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:44.000
to the cloud.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:49.760
We have Pub/Sub, which is a
publish-subscribe mechanism.

00:21:49.760 --> 00:21:54.200
We have Dataflow for batch
and streaming processing,

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:57.860
and Bigtable and
Spanner for storage.

00:21:57.860 --> 00:22:01.610
And then I'm going to show a
little bit a simple chatbot

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:06.260
that we built in a very
quick manner to show,

00:22:06.260 --> 00:22:09.890
once you have the ontology
and these APIs in place,

00:22:09.890 --> 00:22:12.740
how easy it is to
tap into the system

00:22:12.740 --> 00:22:15.800
and extract whatever information
you would like to have.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:22.370
So first, I'm going to
talk about IoT Core.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:24.230
It's a fully-managed
service for you

00:22:24.230 --> 00:22:27.090
to connect your devices
and systems to the cloud.

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:29.180
And as you can see on
the architecture diagram,

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:32.720
you can combine it with
different other components,

00:22:32.720 --> 00:22:36.170
like Pub/Sub, Dataflow, so
you can collect, process,

00:22:36.170 --> 00:22:38.930
and analyze your data.

00:22:38.930 --> 00:22:42.420
So far, in the Bay Area, we
have around 100 buildings

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:43.970
that were onboarded.

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:46.360
And we have around
20,000 devices

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:51.010
that are connected, along
with 120,000 points that are

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:53.530
publishing data to the cloud.

00:22:53.530 --> 00:22:57.100
So once these devices are
connected and are pushing

00:22:57.100 --> 00:23:00.190
telemetry data to
the cloud, we rely

00:23:00.190 --> 00:23:04.380
on Pub/Sub, which is an
enterprise-oriented middleware.

00:23:04.380 --> 00:23:07.300
It's secure, scalable,
and globally distributed,

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:11.950
where you have a topic and
multiple subscriptions that

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:14.770
can collect data for you.

00:23:14.770 --> 00:23:20.260
So far, these devices send
approximately 4 million

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:23.530
messages per day,
which is kind of a lot,

00:23:23.530 --> 00:23:26.170
just to give you an
idea about the scale

00:23:26.170 --> 00:23:28.660
that Pub/Sub can handle.

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:30.430
Once this data
comes into Pub/Sub,

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:33.640
you can see that you can
have different mechanism

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:37.300
to start playing with the
data, do some batch and stream

00:23:37.300 --> 00:23:38.900
processing on it.

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:41.110
And this is where
we use Dataflow,

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:45.340
because it gives you a unified
batch and processing service.

00:23:45.340 --> 00:23:49.060
It relies on Apache Beam, which
is an open source programming

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:49.850
model.

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:53.110
So you write your logic once.

00:23:53.110 --> 00:23:56.135
And then you can
pick whatever running

00:23:56.135 --> 00:23:57.260
environment you would like.

00:23:57.260 --> 00:24:02.260
So it can be Spark, or it
can be, as well, Dataflow.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:05.230
The cool thing about
Dataflow, it's fully managed.

00:24:05.230 --> 00:24:06.880
You don't need to scale it.

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:09.740
And so it's hassle-free.

00:24:09.740 --> 00:24:11.260
And of course,
it's very scalable.

00:24:11.260 --> 00:24:14.770
It supports millions
of queries per second.

00:24:14.770 --> 00:24:18.100
So Dataflow is grabbing
the data from Pub/Sub,

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:20.650
and then doing the
processing on it

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:25.090
and putting it in Bigtable,
which is a fully-managed NoSQL

00:24:25.090 --> 00:24:30.010
database for you that can handle
up to petabytes of data sets.

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:33.460
And it's ideal for
Internet of Things data,

00:24:33.460 --> 00:24:35.620
like the time series.

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:39.910
Currently, we store around
500 gigabytes of data

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:41.660
coming from our buildings.

00:24:41.660 --> 00:24:45.370
And as I mentioned, we have only
100 buildings onboarded today.

00:24:47.890 --> 00:24:51.370
And then my favorite
is Dialogflow.

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:56.090
Dialogflow allows you to build
a chatbot or interactive agent

00:24:56.090 --> 00:25:00.400
that rely on, really,
the knowledge of Google

00:25:00.400 --> 00:25:05.020
when it comes to the machine
learning expertise related

00:25:05.020 --> 00:25:06.230
to NLU.

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:08.050
It supports multiple channels.

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:09.640
You can do chats.

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:11.710
You can do voice.

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:16.340
Whatever channel you think
about, you can integrate it.

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:19.420
And it's one of the
most widely-used tools

00:25:19.420 --> 00:25:22.297
to build actions on top
of the Google Assistant.

00:25:26.120 --> 00:25:28.040
So I will go through
the demo a little bit.

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:37.280
Oh, I can't point?

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:41.300
So as you can see here, we
have a user which can be one

00:25:41.300 --> 00:25:45.110
of these three personas-- it can
be the landlord, the occupant,

00:25:45.110 --> 00:25:48.650
or even the facility
maintenance operator--

00:25:48.650 --> 00:25:53.210
that will interact with
the chat application that

00:25:53.210 --> 00:25:55.910
will head the
facility's bot, which

00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:59.660
will interact with Dialogflow
to interpret whatever the user

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:01.040
input was.

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:03.530
And then the facility's
bot will actually

00:26:03.530 --> 00:26:06.560
handle the call with the APIs.

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:11.060
So I created a
little video demo.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:12.830
Of course, this is
just a proof of concept

00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:14.430
to show what is possible.

00:26:14.430 --> 00:26:18.440
But you can stretch your mind
to support any type of operation

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:21.098
based on the data that
we have in our buildings.

00:26:32.143 --> 00:26:34.060
SPEAKER 1: I'll put it
in full screen for you.

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:34.852
CHARBEL KAED: Yeah.

00:26:34.852 --> 00:26:36.450
I just wanted to
make it high quality.

00:26:36.450 --> 00:26:36.950
Thanks.

00:26:45.160 --> 00:26:50.310
So what you will
see here is the user

00:26:50.310 --> 00:26:56.540
is just typing queries to count
floors on a certain building.

00:26:56.540 --> 00:27:00.310
So the beauty of that is that
once we put this in place,

00:27:00.310 --> 00:27:03.420
we can change the building
and tap to any building

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:05.080
that Google is operating.

00:27:05.080 --> 00:27:07.420
So it can be in
Cambridge, in New York.

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:10.320
It will not change how
we deal with this query.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:12.880
And this is thanks to
the ontologies, but also

00:27:12.880 --> 00:27:16.050
the capacity of Dialogflow
to interpret the query.

00:27:18.930 --> 00:27:22.410
You can search for
meeting rooms information

00:27:22.410 --> 00:27:26.090
such as, I want to count how
many meeting rooms do I have,

00:27:26.090 --> 00:27:29.310
or I want to just see them.

00:27:29.310 --> 00:27:32.033
I want to see the largest
meeting room, because maybe I'm

00:27:32.033 --> 00:27:33.450
hosting an event,
and I would like

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:35.400
to know, where should I book?

00:27:38.430 --> 00:27:40.020
I can change sites.

00:27:40.020 --> 00:27:44.250
And if I'm maintenance
operator, I

00:27:44.250 --> 00:27:46.890
would like to see it
and understand, where

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:51.090
are these HVAC components?

00:27:51.090 --> 00:27:53.220
Because when you are a
maintenance operator,

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:55.800
usually, you are
walking into a building,

00:27:55.800 --> 00:28:00.360
and all of these components
are hidden for you

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:01.770
behind the ceiling.

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:03.150
So you don't see any pumps.

00:28:03.150 --> 00:28:04.620
You don't see any fans.

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:07.210
You don't see any of
these HVAC components.

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:08.020
So you're like, OK.

00:28:08.020 --> 00:28:08.950
I need to find them.

00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:10.197
I need to locate them.

00:28:10.197 --> 00:28:12.030
And this is where as a
maintenance operator,

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:16.080
you can use similar capabilities
with a chatbot to understand,

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:17.370
where is the device?

00:28:17.370 --> 00:28:19.440
Where is this equipment
I need to go replace?

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:20.940
Where is it located?

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:22.920
So here, we're still
targeting the floor level.

00:28:22.920 --> 00:28:24.780
But you can imagine,
we can show a map

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:26.340
and guide the
maintenance operator

00:28:26.340 --> 00:28:30.630
to go and look up where he
needs to look to prepare

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:31.680
the environment for him.

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:37.110
So he can locate a fan.

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:40.890
Because he needs to change
this type of equipment,

00:28:40.890 --> 00:28:42.630
he can group them
by floor because he

00:28:42.630 --> 00:28:46.410
would like to run some
diagnostic operations on them,

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:51.840
and benchmark them by floor,
and how they are behaving.

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:56.400
And here, we can see more
queries related to the HVAC.

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:59.550
So these queries, and the
keywords that you are seeing--

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:02.580
like the fans, the
pumps, the fan coils--

00:29:02.580 --> 00:29:08.410
are all taken from Brick schemas
that we talked about earlier.

00:29:08.410 --> 00:29:12.870
Because Brick allows to lay the
foundation of this vocabulary.

00:29:12.870 --> 00:29:22.410
So we took these vocabularies
and put them here

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:25.950
And of course, for some
who don't want to type,

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:29.460
we have also interactive
menus where these queries can

00:29:29.460 --> 00:29:34.410
be already populated,
and the operator

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:39.597
need just to hit a button
or click on one of them.

00:29:39.597 --> 00:29:40.680
So that's it for the demo.

00:29:43.690 --> 00:29:45.840
So of course, this was
just a proof of concept

00:29:45.840 --> 00:29:52.690
to show what is possible on
top of this building platform.

00:29:57.720 --> 00:29:59.540
I think I'm stuck on--

00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:00.880
OK.

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:01.380
Thanks.

00:30:11.010 --> 00:30:11.770
I think it's OK.

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:13.910
There's just one slide.

00:30:13.910 --> 00:30:16.140
Thanks.

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:18.690
So another example is also
to extract time series

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:21.870
and integrate them
with the chatbot

00:30:21.870 --> 00:30:24.000
or maybe integrate them
in another third-party

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:26.520
application.

00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:29.160
So with that said, I will
pass the mic for Marc

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:30.696
for the conclusion.

00:30:30.696 --> 00:30:31.860
MARC PAWLIGER: Thanks.

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:34.670
Thank you, [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:38.120
Well, I hope that gives you a
little bit of an idea of what

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:41.540
we've been building with this
platform and what it can do.

00:30:41.540 --> 00:30:44.000
This really is a work
in progress for us

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:46.820
for how we manage our
own facilities at Google.

00:30:46.820 --> 00:30:49.550
But it really is
meant to show you

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:53.220
some of the themes that
have been pervasive,

00:30:53.220 --> 00:30:56.210
I think, throughout the
conference here, particularly--

00:30:56.210 --> 00:30:59.180
we're doing this for
ourselves on our own stack.

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:01.010
And it's basically
showing that you

00:31:01.010 --> 00:31:03.590
can build these rich
applications using

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:06.920
a lot of the capabilities
that Google already has.

00:31:06.920 --> 00:31:09.710
So it's modernizing
an infrastructure

00:31:09.710 --> 00:31:13.760
that frankly hasn't been
changed in many decades.

00:31:13.760 --> 00:31:16.700
So you're seeing a
lot of new analytics,

00:31:16.700 --> 00:31:19.190
and applications, and
platforms at the top level,

00:31:19.190 --> 00:31:22.760
but we're going very deep into
this stack all the way down

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:23.960
to the device.

00:31:23.960 --> 00:31:27.080
You're seeing that we're using
these building blocks in GCP

00:31:27.080 --> 00:31:30.620
as basically a way to build
an application platform.

00:31:30.620 --> 00:31:33.800
So you're utilizing and not
having to reinvent the wheel--

00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:35.270
as Charbel mentioned--

00:31:35.270 --> 00:31:37.220
for a lot of the
capabilities in building out

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:38.480
these applications.

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:41.810
And we're also going
from analytics to action.

00:31:41.810 --> 00:31:44.660
So we're using capabilities
like machine learning.

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:47.420
We're using a lot of our data
visualization and so forth

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:50.070
to be able to take
that raw data--

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:52.430
which is sort of difficult
to maybe discern messaging

00:31:52.430 --> 00:31:53.810
and action from--

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:55.730
and do the analysis
to be able to come up

00:31:55.730 --> 00:31:58.700
with a punch list for
techs or operators

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:01.945
to be able to go take care of.

00:32:01.945 --> 00:32:02.445
Whoop.

00:32:02.445 --> 00:32:05.290
Went the wrong way.

00:32:05.290 --> 00:32:07.790
Our onboarding
process really is,

00:32:07.790 --> 00:32:09.560
I think, leaps and
bounds ahead of what

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:12.560
it takes to do onboarding
in typical systems today.

00:32:12.560 --> 00:32:14.840
And this is where a
lot of the intelligence

00:32:14.840 --> 00:32:16.670
is being developed right now.

00:32:16.670 --> 00:32:20.970
As we go into new buildings and
discover new types of devices,

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:24.050
new kinds of network topologies,
new kinds of gateway boxes

00:32:24.050 --> 00:32:26.882
and so forth, that's helping
to make this platform richer so

00:32:26.882 --> 00:32:28.340
that the next time
we encounter it,

00:32:28.340 --> 00:32:30.080
it's much quicker to
be able to onboard.

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:34.580
It's really building out
the fundamental intelligence

00:32:34.580 --> 00:32:37.400
of the underlying systems so
that it actually applies down

00:32:37.400 --> 00:32:40.040
the road as we go along.

00:32:40.040 --> 00:32:42.200
And we're using
structured data here.

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:46.850
So we're trying to use the best
of what the industry is working

00:32:46.850 --> 00:32:50.330
on right now in
terms of ontologies,

00:32:50.330 --> 00:32:53.330
in terms of terminologies,
so that an operator will

00:32:53.330 --> 00:32:57.110
be able to take lessons that
they learned in one building

00:32:57.110 --> 00:32:59.270
and be able to apply them
into an entire campus,

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:03.060
or other buildings as well.

00:33:03.060 --> 00:33:04.070
So what next?

00:33:04.070 --> 00:33:05.692
Well, that's where
you guys come in.

00:33:05.692 --> 00:33:08.150
We'd really like to continue
the conversation, particularly

00:33:08.150 --> 00:33:11.000
if you have device data,
or interesting ways

00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:11.980
of representing data.

00:33:11.980 --> 00:33:14.780
So we touched mostly on
things like climate control,

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:16.130
HVAC today.

00:33:16.130 --> 00:33:17.960
But I mentioned that
there's a lot of work

00:33:17.960 --> 00:33:20.090
that we're also doing
for things like occupancy

00:33:20.090 --> 00:33:24.180
sensors, and alarms,
and physical security,

00:33:24.180 --> 00:33:24.962
and so forth.

00:33:24.962 --> 00:33:26.420
So if those are
the kind of devices

00:33:26.420 --> 00:33:27.870
that you work with,
we'd like to know,

00:33:27.870 --> 00:33:29.703
how would that integrate
with this platform?

00:33:29.703 --> 00:33:31.280
What would you be
able to do if you

00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:33.770
didn't have to do all
of that plumbing down

00:33:33.770 --> 00:33:36.440
at the bottom of the stack,
but were able to just apply

00:33:36.440 --> 00:33:41.170
whatever it is that you do best
at the top for applications?

00:33:41.170 --> 00:33:44.300
On device security
and maintenance--

00:33:44.300 --> 00:33:46.580
I touched on this a little
bit at the beginning,

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:50.660
but we have been talking in the
open source community about how

00:33:50.660 --> 00:33:54.890
to do continuous integrated
testing of your devices

00:33:54.890 --> 00:33:57.980
to make sure that they're only
communicating with the devices

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:00.900
that they need to in the way
that they're prescribed to.

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:03.650
And that's something that we'll
be talking a little bit about

00:34:03.650 --> 00:34:04.753
more publicly.

00:34:04.753 --> 00:34:06.170
But essentially,
this is something

00:34:06.170 --> 00:34:08.420
that we want to do
at Google as well

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:12.989
to be able to ensure that our
network remains secure as well.

00:34:12.989 --> 00:34:16.250
And then, again, the third party
apps and other integration.

00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:17.750
Partner involvement
and so forth.

00:34:17.750 --> 00:34:20.989
Remaining involved with
the various standards,

00:34:20.989 --> 00:34:24.250
bodies, like ASHRAE, Brick,
Haystack, and so forth.

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:26.000
And if you have
involvement there as well,

00:34:26.000 --> 00:34:28.400
we'd certainly like
to hear whether or not

00:34:28.400 --> 00:34:32.485
what you're doing integrates
well with what we're building

00:34:32.485 --> 00:34:34.610
with this stack, so that
we can make sure that when

00:34:34.610 --> 00:34:36.780
we encounter your devices
out in the field--

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:38.295
hopefully we will--
that we would

00:34:38.295 --> 00:34:39.920
be able to integrate
with them as well.

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:42.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

